The table and column structures relevant for the views and tasks are as follows:

- employees.department: id (char), dept_name (varchar)
- employees.department_employee: employee_id (bigint), department_id (char), from_date (date), to_date (date)
- employees.department_manager: employee_id (bigint), department_id (char), from_date (date), to_date (date)
- employees.employee: id (bigint), first_name, last_name, hire_date, gender, birth_date
- employees.salary: employee_id (bigint), amount (bigint), from_date (date), to_date (date)
- employees.title: employee_id (bigint), title (varchar), from_date (date), to_date (date)

Department employees and managers have from_date and to_date for validity periods to identify current ones.
Salaries and titles also have from_date and to_date for historic records.

I will create the first materialized view `exec_department_summary` using these tables and considering only current active employees and current salaries, as well as current manager.

Proceeding to create the first materialized view now.
| execute_sql {"sql": "CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW employees.exec_department_summary AS\nSELECT d.dept_name AS department_name,\n       COUNT(de.employee_id) AS total_employees,\n       AVG(s.amount)::numeric(10,2) AS avg_salary,\n       SUM(s.amount)::bigint AS total_payroll,\n       CONCAT(e.first_name, ' ', e.last_name) AS manager_name\nFROM employees.department d\nLEFT JOIN employees.department_employee de ON de.department_id = d.id AND de.to_date = '9999-01-01'\nLEFT JOIN employees.salary s ON s.employee_id = de.employee_id AND s.to_date = '9999-01-01'\nLEFT JOIN employees.department_manager dm ON dm.department_id = d.id AND dm.to_date = '9999-01-01'\nLEFT JOIN employees.employee e ON e.id = dm.employee_id\nGROUP BY d.dept_name, e.first_name, e.last_name;"}
The first materialized view `exec_department_summary` has been created successfully.

Next, I will create the second materialized view `exec_hiring_trends` with the columns:
- hire_year
- employees_hired
- avg_starting_salary
- retention_rate (percentage still employed)
- top_hiring_department

I'll base it on the employees' hire_date, joining with salary and department_employee tables for starting salary and retention, also grouping by hire year. 
Proceeding with this now.
| execute_sql {"sql": "CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW employees.exec_hiring_trends AS\nWITH hires AS (\n    SELECT EXTRACT(YEAR FROM e.hire_date)::int AS hire_year, \n           e.id AS employee_id,\n           s.amount AS starting_salary,\n           de.department_id\n    FROM employees.employee e\n    LEFT JOIN employees.salary s ON s.employee_id = e.id AND s.from_date = e.hire_date\n    LEFT JOIN employees.department_employee de ON de.employee_id = e.id AND de.from_date <= e.hire_date AND de.to_date >= e.hire_date\n),\nretain AS (\n    SELECT hire_year,\n           COUNT(employee_id) AS employees_hired,\n           AVG(starting_salary)::numeric(10,2) AS avg_starting_salary,\n           SUM(CASE WHEN e.id IS NOT NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)::float / COUNT(h.employee_id)::float AS retention_rate\n    FROM hires h\n    LEFT JOIN employees.employee e ON e.id = h.employee_id AND e.hire_date <= '9999-01-01' -- still employed check\n    GROUP BY hire_year\n),\ntop_department AS (\n    SELECT hire_year, department_id,\n           RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY hire_year ORDER BY COUNT(employee_id) DESC) AS dept_rank\n    FROM hires\n    GROUP BY hire_year, department_id\n)\nSELECT r.hire_year,\n       r.employees_hired,\n       r.avg_starting_salary,\n       (r.retention_rate * 100)::numeric(5,2) AS retention_rate,\n       d.dept_name AS top_hiring_department\nFROM retain r\nLEFT JOIN top_department td ON r.hire_year = td.hire_year AND td.dept_rank = 1\nLEFT JOIN employees.department d ON d.id = td.department_id;"}
The second materialized view `exec_hiring_trends` has been created successfully.

Now, I will create the third materialized view `exec_salary_distribution` with columns:
- salary_band (defined ranges)
- employee_count
- percentage_of_workforce
- most_common_title

This view will classify employees into salary bands based on their current salaries, count them, calculate percentage of total workforce, and find the most frequent job title in each band.

Proceeding with the creation of this view now.
| execute_sql {"sql": "CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW employees.exec_salary_distribution AS\nWITH current_salaries AS (\n    SELECT s.employee_id, s.amount, t.title\n    FROM employees.salary s\n    LEFT JOIN employees.title t ON t.employee_id = s.employee_id AND t.to_date = '9999-01-01'\n    WHERE s.to_date = '9999-01-01'\n),\nbanded AS (\n    SELECT employee_id, amount, title,\n    CASE\n        WHEN amount BETWEEN 30000 AND 49999 THEN '30K-50K'\n        WHEN amount BETWEEN 50000 AND 69999 THEN '50K-70K'\n        WHEN amount BETWEEN 70000 AND 89999 THEN '70K-90K'\n        WHEN amount BETWEEN 90000 AND 109999 THEN '90K-110K'\n        ELSE '110K+'\n    END AS salary_band\n    FROM current_salaries\n),\ntotal_count AS (\n    SELECT COUNT(*) AS total FROM current_salaries\n),\nband_stats AS (\n    SELECT salary_band,\n           COUNT(employee_id) AS employee_count,\n           ROUND( (COUNT(employee_id)::numeric / (SELECT total FROM total_count)) * 100, 2) AS percentage_of_workforce,\n           (SELECT title FROM banded bb WHERE bb.salary_band = b.salary_band GROUP BY title ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC LIMIT 1) AS most_common_title\n    FROM banded b\n    GROUP BY salary_band\n)\nSELECT salary_band, employee_count, percentage_of_workforce, most_common_title FROM band_stats;"}
All three materialized views have been created successfully:
- exec_department_summary
- exec_hiring_trends
- exec_salary_distribution

Next, I will create the stored procedure `generate_monthly_report(report_date DATE)`. This procedure will:
- create a `monthly_reports` table if not exists
- insert one summary record for the current stats on the report_date provided
- return the generated report_id

Proceeding to create the procedure now.
| execute_sql {"sql": "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS employees.monthly_reports (\n  report_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  report_date DATE NOT NULL,\n  department_count INTEGER NOT NULL,\n  total_employees INTEGER NOT NULL,\n  avg_salary NUMERIC(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  generated_at TIMESTAMP WITHOUT TIME ZONE DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\n);"}
| execute_sql {"sql": "CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION employees.generate_monthly_report(report_date DATE) RETURNS INTEGER AS $$\nDECLARE\n  v_report_id INTEGER;\n  v_department_count INTEGER;\n  v_total_employees INTEGER;\n  v_avg_salary NUMERIC(10,2);\nBEGIN\n  SELECT COUNT(*) INTO v_department_count FROM employees.department;\n  SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT de.employee_id) INTO v_total_employees\n    FROM employees.department_employee de\n    WHERE de.to_date = '9999-01-01';\n  SELECT AVG(s.amount)::numeric(10,2) INTO v_avg_salary\n    FROM employees.salary s\n    WHERE s.to_date = '9999-01-01';\n\n  INSERT INTO employees.monthly_reports(report_date, department_count, total_employees, avg_salary)\n  VALUES (report_date, v_department_count, v_total_employees, v_avg_salary) RETURNING report_id INTO v_report_id;\n\n  RETURN v_report_id;\nEND;\n$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;"}
Stored procedure `generate_monthly_report(report_date DATE)` created successfully.

Next, I will create the trigger `high_salary_alert` which fires on insertions of salary records where amount > 120000, inserting alerts into `salary_alerts` table.

First, I will create the `salary_alerts` table if it doesn't exist.

Proceeding with these tasks now.
| execute_sql {"sql": "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS employees.salary_alerts (\n  alert_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  employee_id BIGINT NOT NULL,\n  salary_amount BIGINT NOT NULL,\n  alert_date TIMESTAMP WITHOUT TIME ZONE DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  status VARCHAR(20) DEFAULT 'new'\n);"}
| execute_sql {"sql": "CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION employees.fn_high_salary_alert() RETURNS TRIGGER AS $$\nBEGIN\n  IF NEW.amount > 120000 THEN\n    INSERT INTO employees.salary_alerts(employee_id, salary_amount, alert_date, status)\n    VALUES (NEW.employee_id, NEW.amount, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, 'new');\n  END IF;\n  RETURN NEW;\nEND;\n$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;"}
| execute_sql {"sql": "DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS high_salary_alert ON employees.salary;"}
